return {
  summary = "True-colour, frame-animated battle sprites for the whole national dex, loaded from the DBK animated solo-sprite art shipped in the mod's own assets/ folder -- local only, no network.",
  author = "Gen1Recomp Mod Studio",
  tags = { "sprite", "battle", "graphics", "gen1", "gen2" },
  differences = {
    added = {
      "Every battle front/back pic the mod can resolve is drawn from the DBK animated sprite sheet instead of the game's 4-shade pic: the sheet is one horizontal frame strip, cropped and resampled in memory per frame and played as a loop.",
      "The gen-1 Pokemon Tower ghost is left to the engine. Until the Silph Scope is in the bag, every tower wild battle -- and the scripted ghost Marowak -- is disguised as a \"GHOST\", and this mod paints nothing over that disguise: the enemy keeps the game's own ghost.png, at the game's own scale, with no true-colour mark. The disguise is identified from the engine's own battle state (battle.ghost / scopeReveal / ghostReveal), never from the species, so a randomizer that replaced the mon underneath is covered too, and the real sprite returns in the pack's art once the Scope unveils it. Gen 1 only: Gold has no tower ghost, and no layout battle scene runs the scope-less ghost.",
      "National-dex form conventions: regional forms, megas, gigantamax and gender-distinct forms each resolve to their own sheet (CHARIZARD_MEGA_X -> CHARIZARD_1, RAICHU_ALOLA -> RAICHU_1, ALCREMIE_GMAX -> ALCREMIE_63, MEOWSTIC_FEMALE -> MEOWSTIC_1, ABOMASNOW female -> ABOMASNOW_female, ...); cosmetic-only forms with no sheet of their own fall back to their base species.",
      "Lookup is local-only from the mod's own assets/front, assets/front_shiny, assets/back and assets/back_shiny folders -- the sole source; a sheet the pack has no art for leaves the vanilla pic. The full DBK sheet set ships in those folders with the mod (no network); CREDITS.md names the artists.",
      "Image fidelity comes first. Every sheet is baked at the largest scale its picture box can hold (SPRITE SIZE = MAX DETAIL, the default): kept at 1:1 where it already fits, so a small Pokemon keeps every source pixel, and shrunk no further than it must be where it does not, so a big Pokemon fills the box instead of sitting at half size inside it. SMALL / NORMAL / BIG instead divide every sheet by one whole number for strictly proportional sizes. GROUND ANCHOR (FEET, the default) then stands every grounded sprite's own feet on the bottom of its picture, so all grounded Pokemon share one ground line however much empty padding their sheets carry (the pack does not trim its sheets, so Zigzagoon's feet start far higher up its sheet than Wooloo's -- yet both must land on the same line); PACK uses the pack's own SpeciesMetrics offsets (data/dbk_metrics.lua) and BOX bottom-centres with no alignment. A sprite the pack draws airborne (a flying/levitating species, data/dbk_float.lua) is instead lifted off that line by 35% of its own height by the FLOATERS row, so a big flyer hovers higher than a small one.",
      "Baking always samples the nearest source pixel (never an average), so the pack's exact colours and hard pixel edges survive at every size and each sprite reads as crisp pixel art, not a soft, blurry downscale.",
      "The Pokedex front pic is replaced too, on both generations: the dex loads its own front-pic path rather than drawing through a battle screen, so a dedicated dex hook hands it the same baked frame (true-colour). On gen1 that hook reaches the national dex's OWN Pokédex screen (national_dex's page strip over src.ui.DexEntryMenu), including its STATS page and its LEFT/RIGHT alternate-form browsing: the sheet follows the form the screen is showing, not merely the species the entry was opened for. The DEX SPRITES row turns it off.",
      "The party SUMMARY screen -- the one the party menu's STATS action opens, which is the screen the national dex repaints the stats box of -- shows the same animated front pic on both generations: on the native SummaryMenu (gen1's and gen2's) and on any custom screen that replaces it under the same 'SummaryMenu' id. A replacement class pushed under that id, or any other pushed screen painting a mon's own front pic (the g9 battle engine's wide modern STATS screen, for example), is found by a screen.pushed listener and decorated in place (its own picture swapped for the current frame) rather than disabled or replaced; a screen under the party summary's id uses its own 160x144 picture slot on both pages and is recognised by the id alone, so the national dex's stats-box repaint still composes underneath. The FRONT sheet, or the *_shiny FRONT sheet for a shiny Pokemon, resolved through the same species/form/gender mapping the battle pics use. The SUMMARY SPRITES row turns it off. A screen that paints its OWN page -- g9-gui's 540x360 ADV.STATS panel -- is reached by neither hook, so it asks for the same frame through the drawSummaryFrame(mon, box) export and wears it in the window's top-left corner on every page.",
      "The SUMMARY screen's picture can be drawn at REAL SIZE (the default) or shrunk with the SUMMARY SIZE row -- 75%, 50% or 25% of the same baked frame, still animated and still true-colour. This row governs the SUMMARY only: battles, the Pokedex and every custom battle screen keep the full-size picture whatever it says.",
      "The party list's little 16x16 icons are replaced too, on both generations, from the mod's own bundled animated icon atlas (assets/icons/party_icons.png, indexed by data/icon_data.lua): two true-colour frames per Pokemon, taken from the third-party 'icones animados' icon pack and point-sampled 4:1 to the icon slot at build time, with the pack's female variant where it has one. An icon is a Quad over the one decoded atlas -- no per-mon bake. gen1 marks the icon rect true-colour for the SGB/GBC zone pass; gen2 draws raw in GBC mode and reproduces the held-item marker in the icon's bottom-left tile. An egg uses the pack's own egg icon; any species the pack has no entry for keeps the game's own icon. The PARTY ICONS row turns it off.",
      "The whole party page can optionally be redrawn from the game's OWN parts on a 640x576 surface (4x the Game Boy screen, filling the window): the same two-line rows (name, level, HP figures, HP-bar tiles, status/FNT), cursor, CANCEL row and box, from the tiles and fonts of whichever generation you are playing, at exactly the same proportions because it is literally the game's own draw run under a 4x transform. The 4x page is what lets the high-resolution icon atlas (assets/icons/party_icons_hd.png, the same icons packed at the pack's natural 64x64 frames, same cell order as the 16x16 atlas) be drawn 1:1 in each row's 16x16 design slot instead of being shrunk. The action list that opens when a Pokemon is chosen (STATS / SWITCH / field moves / ITEM / CANCEL) is the game's own submenu, unchanged, and a classic screen pushed over the page (the summary, a message box) is dressed in place and drawn on the same 4x page. Off by default; the G9 PARTY SCREEN row turns it on.",
      "A Pokemon that has Terastallized wears a cut-crystal film tinted with its Tera type: the type's own colour, cut into a shattered pane of flat angular facets by a voronoi diagram sampled from the bundled assets/tera_crystal.png pattern -- freely scattered sites, so the shards come out in a chaotic mix of broad slabs and small chips with no rows or columns of seams left to read as a grid. Each shard is held inside a size band: chips below the floor are folded into their neighbours and plates above the ceiling are split along a wavy fracture, so the mean shard is small without any single shard shrinking to a speck or growing into one dominating plate. The pane is edged with fine dark bevel seams that carry the type's tint, inside a chamfered glowing rim, with whole-facet specular glints and small white 4-pointed star sparkles -- a solid white heart with four tapering, translucent arms and nothing on the diagonals -- made only of light laid over the facets, blended 70/30 into the frame (the TERA TRANSPARENCY row tunes the crystal's own opacity, from an almost solid gem at 5% to a sheer, glassy film at 60%), drawn ONLY where the sprite's own pixels are opaque so the creature's true silhouette is covered and never the transparent margin of its frame. The star sparkles are their own animation, each blinking on its own cycle across the crystal's clearest, brightest facets (and scaled up with a large bake so the sparkle is never swallowed), and the whole crystal -- facets, bevels, rim and stars -- rides the sprite's own animation, shifting with the creature's motion frame to frame rather than staying nailed to the picture box. The Tera state is read live from the battle_forms mod; TERA ART turns the film off.",
      "A Pokemon that is Dynamaxed or Gigantamaxed right now grows through a fixed ladder of real-time size phases -- x1 -> 1.05 (0.3s) -> 1.10 (0.4s) -> 1.20 (0.5s) -> 1.50 (0.8s) -- where each phase eases IN on a parabolic curve, gentle at the start and leaping just before the next phase begins. When the transformation ends it does not snap back: the mod records how far up the ladder the mon had climbed and walks the very same phases back down, at the same durations and the same curve, so the descent is the climb in reverse and the whole transformation reads forward and then backwards (a full 1.50 -> 1.00 fall takes the same 2.00s the climb did, and a mon that reverted while still small shrinks for exactly as long as it grew). The factor is a pure function of elapsed time, so it is identical on every machine and cannot desync from the sprite clock. It is applied at the same two seams the rest of the mod uses: on gen1 by wrapping the engine's own drawBattlerPic and scaling about the picture's bottom-centre -- feet stay planted, the creature grows upward -- and on gen2 through the pack's own picScale seam. The live Dynamax/Gigantamax state is read from the battle_forms mod, or from a wild raid boss's declaration; the mod also publishes that state to a custom battle scene through mod.exports.dynamaxStateOf(mon, battler) = {active, known, grow, shrinking}, so g9-Battle-Scene's DYNAMAX FIELD FX ramp their darkened field and red aura with grow and hold a fainted Dynamaxed mon on screen -- still shrinking -- until known clears, only then bursting it. DYNAMAX GROW turns the ladder off.",
      "Every battle battler also casts a contact shadow, so a Pokemon no longer floats unsupported on the field. The shadow art is bundled (assets/shadow/1.png, 2.png and 3.png -- the small/medium/large contact shadows from La Base de Sky's Graphics/Pokemon/Shadow folder) and is composited BEHIND the sprite, never as a second draw pass, so it rides the same frame and can never flicker with the frame clock. A species is resolved the way Essentials' own shadow_filename does it: a file named for the sheet's stem (assets/shadow/<STEM>.png) wins, and otherwise the generic art is chosen by the pack's own ShadowSize (data/dbk_metrics.lua), clamped to the three files, with ShadowSize 0 meaning that species casts none. The shadow width tracks the pack's ShadowSize (0.55x the sprite at the default 1, 0.65x at 2, 0.50x at -1, and so on) and the pack's ShadowSprite x offset nudges it sideways; its ally/enemy y values are deliberately ignored, because a baked shadow is always pinned to the ground line -- a floating Pokemon's shadow belongs on the ground, not on the Pokemon. The BATTLE SHADOWS row turns it off; the native battle screens and every custom battle scene both get it.",
      "Mod Manager rows: BATTLE SPRITES, ANIMATE, SPRITE SPEED, SHINY ART, FEMALE ART, SPRITE SIZE, GROUND ANCHOR, FLOATERS, BATTLE SHADOWS, DEX SPRITES, SUMMARY SPRITES, SUMMARY SIZE, PARTY ICONS, G9 PARTY SCREEN, TERA ART, TERA TRANSPARENCY, DYNAMAX GROW.",
    },
    known = {
      "The mod ships its full DBK battle-sprite art (assets/front, front_shiny, back, back_shiny), the party-icon atlases (assets/icons/party_icons.png and party_icons_hd.png) and the effect art (assets/tera_crystal.png, assets/egg.png, assets/shadow/*.png) in its own assets/ folder, and never touches the network. The sprite and icon art is community fan art: the EeveeExpo Resource Pack (Luka S.J., the Smogon sprite projects and the many named artists), La Base de Sky's DBK pack and the 'icones animados' icon pack are all credited in CREDITS.md, with licence notes in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.",
      "TERA ART's Tera state comes from the optional battle_forms mod; with battle_forms absent, or for a type with no colour (???) , nothing is drawn over the sprite and the battle is unaffected.",
      "DYNAMAX GROW's live state is the same battle_forms mod, or the kind a wild Dynamax/Gigantamax raid boss declares; with neither, no mon grows and the battle is unaffected. No artwork is involved -- the growth is pure geometry applied to the baked frame's draw -- so the effect needs nothing installed and cannot fail to load.",
      "Battle pics, every Pokedex front pic (gen1's entry page -- including the national dex's own Pokédex screen and its STATS page -- and gen2's entry page and list), every party Summary screen's front pic (the native screen and any custom screen replacing it) and the party list's 16x16 icons are replaced; other UI graphics are not. With G9 PARTY SCREEN on the party page itself is additionally presented on a 640x576 surface (the game's own draw at 4x, so the icons show at natural size) -- no other screen is enlarged by it.",
      "No hard dependency: national_dex (the species-id source the form map is built against), the g9 battle engine and its custom battle scene are all optional. Species resolve against the running game's own dex, so the mod works with national_dex absent as well -- a species only national_dex knows simply never appears in play -- and the summary/custom-screen hooks are simply dormant when the engine/scene are absent.",
      "The high-resolution 64x64 icon frames are also published for OTHER mods, whether or not G9 PARTY SCREEN is on: mod.find(\"g9-battle-sprites\").exports.iconArtHD(mon, frame) answers (quads, image, cellPixels, box) -- the per-cell quad table, the decoded HD atlas, the frame size (64) and the {x,y,w,h} bounding box of the frame's opaque pixels -- or nil when the pack has no icon for that Pokemon, or on the first call of a session while the atlas is still decoding. The box exists because the frames are bottom-anchored with transparent rows above the creature, so a head-space crop must anchor to the art and not to the frame's top; it is nil when the pixels cannot be read or the whole cell is opaque. The optional 0-based frame index picks one of the pack's two frames per icon (that frame's quad and box); omitted or nil it answers the LIVE frame, exactly as it always did. g9-gui ('modern UI & stats') draws these frames as its party roster's portrait cards, and its ICONS-only PARTY PORTRAITS animation asks for an explicit frame on its own one-second clock; the internal iconArt the party page itself uses is option-gated and is not the same thing.",
      "Three more always-on exports hand other mods the pack's own art directly, whatever this mod's battle options are set to: mod.find(\"g9-battle-sprites\").exports.frontArt(mon) answers (image, width, height, box) -- the Pokemon's FRONT sheet (assets/front/<STEM>.png) baked to frame 1 ALONE, trimmed to that frame's own pixels, as a standalone frame Image at 1:1, so a caller cropping the head can treat the frame's top as the creature's top; box is the frame's own content box ({x=0,y=0,w,h}, the whole image, because the trim IS the content) and only the new frame-1 bake answers it, so a caller can tell it apart from an older copy's whole-animation union bake -- which left frame 1 dozens of transparent rows below the box's top (DRAGONITE's Mega frame has 51), so a card cropping from the top landed on blank rows -- or (nil, pending) while the sheet is still baking, or nil when the pack has no sheet for that species; from 3.5.1 it also answers a FIFTH value, head -- the height, in the frame image's own rows, of a leading head DECORATION (Kingambit's blade, Sirfetchd's leek, Aegislash's hilt), or 0 when the frame's top IS the head, so a caller can start its portrait window BELOW the decoration and show the face; head is measured from the ART, never a species list (a leading run of rows taller than it is wide and at least a fifth of the frame), so short narrow tips (Pikachu's ear tips, Onix's rock tip) and every genuinely TALL creature (Eternatus, Lapras, Wailord, Dragonite, Milotic, Lugia, Giratina, Alakazam, Samurott) answer 0; an older copy answers four values, so head is absent and a caller keeps the old top-anchored crop; exports.iconArt16(mon, frame) answers (quads, image, cellPixels) -- the per-cell quad table (with `full`), the decoded 16x16 icon atlas and the cell size (16) -- for the pack's own assets/icons/party_icons.png, its optional frame index working as iconArtHD's does; and exports.drawSummaryFrame(mon, box) paints the mon's LIVE animation frame straight into a caller-chosen box ({x, w, bottom}, in the caller's own coordinate space: centred across it, feet on box.bottom, mirrored like the engine's summary picture), answering true when it drew and false -- never an error -- when SUMMARY SPRITES is off, the pack has no sheet for that mon, or the sheet is still baking. drawSummaryFrame is for a screen that paints its OWN page and so is reached by neither summary hook: g9-gui's ADV.STATS panel asks for it over the window's top-left corner. g9-gui uses frontArt for its 'BATTLE SPRITES' portraits and the 64x64 HD party cell (iconArtHD) at its real 1:1 size for its 'ICONS' portraits, with the 16x16 atlas (iconArt16) only as the fitted fallback for a copy of this mod that has no HD cell; its ICONS-only PARTY PORTRAITS animation asks those two exports for an explicit frame once a second, so each portrait steps at that rate rather than this mod's sprite FPS.",
      "BATTLE SHADOWS uses the game's own small/medium/large contact-shadow art rather than a per-Pokemon shadow drawing, because the DBK solo-sprite pack ships none (the pack's own games squash the battler's frame into a soft blob, which the mod cannot reproduce from a single sheet); the pack's per-species ShadowSize and ShadowSprite x offset are still honoured, and an assets/shadow/<STEM>.png file overrides the generic art for that species. Note that g9-Battle-Scene already draws its own primitive ellipse shadow under each battler when a background image is present, so with BATTLE SHADOWS on and a background up, a scene battle shows both; turn BATTLE SHADOWS off if you prefer the scene's own.",
      "Female art only applies where the base game exposes a mon's gender.",
      "an unofficial fan mod: Pokemon and all related names and assets are (c) Nintendo / Creatures Inc. / GAME FREAK inc. / The Pokemon Company and this project is not affiliated with or endorsed by them; the DBK, 'icones animados' and La Base de Sky art are third-party fan art under their own terms -- see THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md",
    },
  },
  compat = { engine = ">=0.0.0-0 <2.0.0", modApi = 2 },
}
